From 9c6c76d4eb7bcd331d154203000f28e7eb36d22c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arena-agent <arena-agent@local>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 11:55:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fail an abandoned SAP setup instead of orphaning it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A third report from the field came back at stage: setup after more than 500
seconds with no error, and said that navigating to another page and back
restarted the count. Both point at one bug.

The add-account form holds its device id in useState(() => generateDeviceId()),
so remounting it yields a different one. A signer is bound to its hardware id,
so prepareSigner() took the rebuild branch and called reset() — which rejects
the pending sign requests but never touches settleSetup, where the setup
promise lives. It only nulled `ready`. The caller in authenticate() was left
holding a promise nothing would ever settle, so the screen sat on "initialising
the signer" until the 15 minute guard fired. The worker had been terminate()d
cleanly, so onerror never fired either. That is the mechanism behind the wait.

Three changes:

- reset() now fails settleSetup explicitly, so an abandoned setup reports
  instead of hanging.
- The count's start moves from component state into the store as
  setupStartedAt, so navigating away and back shows how long setup has really
  been running. A wait nine minutes in should not read as one that just began.
- The report gains a setupSeconds line. Setup is the only stage with no
  percentage, and every published timing for it is desktop or Node, so
  "stage: setup" alone cannot answer the "is it stuck?" that the last three
  reports arrived asking.

Left alone deliberately: the device id regenerating per mount is intentional —
one device per account, with a regenerate button beside it. It does mean
navigating rebuilds the signer and repeats the whole setup. That is now a
wasted wait rather than a permanent one, and persisting it is a product
decision rather than a bug fix.

Each fix was verified by reverting it: the orphaned-promise test times out at
15s without the reject, and the elapsed test fails with 'expected +0 to be 120'
without the store-backed start.
